* START-DESCRIPTION:
*
*    call $netfile(op, a, b, c, out, rc)
*
* The operation codes, rc and the limits are in netfile.h; this text is what is behind them.  Nothing here stores anything locally.  A write,
* delete, clear or lock is REFUSED (message 12506) without a byte being sent unless the definition of the server says mode write, which only
* SDSYS can set (set.server): high security is delivered and the administrator loosens it.  The mode is read at NF$OPEN and kept per handle,
* and the definition is read AGAIN at every change or lock, so a handle needs both to say write and the stricter wins.
*
* ORDER OF THE CHECKS ON NF$OPEN, every one of them local and BEFORE ANYTHING IS DIALLED (an internal program is exempt from the kernel's
* gates, so the handler asks for itself - the S.63 lesson).  A refusal is audited as   netfile refused server=<name> reason=<why>:
*     1  the NET gate or the administrator (kernel K$NET; 12465)                                             reason=gate
*     2  the server name is well formed and the server is DEFINED (!netdefs; 12457, 12435) and its definition
*        can be used (host, port, account and key in the shape set.server stores; 12497)                     reason=server
*     3  this ACCOUNT is in the definition's accounts list, case blind; the administrator is exempt (12466)  reason=list
*     4  the mode: not a refusal.  The definition's mode (write exactly; anything else is read) is kept for the handle, reported to the kernel
*        in rc<7> and audited (mode=write) on the open line
*     5  a stored login exists for this person and was made for the definition's key ($netcred READ with the pin;
*        its errors are given as they are, 12463 and 12464 as 12501)                                         reason=credential
*     6  at most 10 servers per process and 100 remote files open (12498, 12510)                             reason=limit
*     7  the remote file name is well formed (12496)                                                         reason=file
* Only then:   netfile open server=<name> file=<remote file>   (with  mode=write  added for a write-mode server) is audited, once, and the
* connection is made (a server this process is
* already connected to is reused: nothing is dialled, but all seven checks were made again).  No password, key or hash is ever audited,
* printed or kept; the keys the login is made from are copied out of the credential, handed to the class and cleared at once.
* Read, readv, recordlocked (a write-mode handle only), select and fileinfo ask the gate again and want the handle to belong to THIS ACCOUNT, so a handle opened before
* a LOGTO is no use after it (the kernel is to call NF$CLOSEALL at LOGTO as well).  CLOSE, CLOSEALL and the refused write operations
* send nothing that needs the gate; the refused ones still want a handle of this account.  Write, delete, clear and lock ask the gate and the
* mode again and then, in this order: that no transaction is open (12515), that the record id and the lock kind are well formed (12508, 12517),
* the audit line (first write, first delete of a handle, every clear; locks are not audited; no id and no record is ever audited), and the
* request.  A changing request that gets NO REPLY is never retried and is not known to have happened or not: 12514 and the audit line
* netfile lost server=<name> op=<operation>.  A lock the server cannot give at once is NF$RC.LOCKED; THE KERNEL LOOPS to wait for it (this
* program only ever sends the no-wait form, so that a wait never looks like a lost connection and the break key works).
*
* CONNECTIONS.  One object of the class !sdclient per server, in the named common block $netfile (so it lives for the process and is
* released with it).  THE OBJECT IN COMMON HAS BEEN MEASURED (19 Oct 26): gplbld/sandbox-netfile.py reads NF$STATE after every connect and sees VARTYPE 15 (an object)
* for the server's element, and every remote file open of the VM witness of 18 Oct lived on it.  Not measured: the whole of the kernel's release of it at the end of a
* process other than by the rows that end a session.  What the kernel source says about it: an object is a reference
* counted OBJDATA in a descriptor of type OBJ (VARTYPE 15); assigning it to an element of a common array copies the descriptor and counts a
* reference, so it survives the end of this program (a common block is on process.named_common and is freed only by DELETE.COMMON
* or at the end of the process).  What this program does about it, so that a failure is SAID and not covered over:
*     - it reads VARTYPE of the element straight after storing it and refuses the open (12504) if that is not an object;
*     - it reads VARTYPE of the element before every use; a connection whose object is no longer an object is dropped and the call
*       answers 12504, naming the server;
*     - NF$STATE shows, for each server, the VARTYPE of its element (15 is an object) and the counters, for a test.
* The server name, the handle table (handle -> server slot, file number on the server, remote file name, account) and the counters are
* plain values in the same block, so what a handle meant can be told even when the object is gone.
*
* A LOST CONNECTION.  The class marks nothing itself: a request that gets no reply (peer gone, reset, NETREPLY seconds with no data) returns its
* failure value and leaves ServerStatus alone.  So ServerStatus is set to the mark NF$NOREPLY before every request, and a reply (any reply) overwrites
* it with the status field of its header (a signed 32 bit value, so it CAN be negative: the loss is the mark still there, tested by equality, never by sign).  A reply
* whose length is not a length (K8c) is the same loss: the class fails the call before it touches ServerStatus.  On a loss the object is
* released (its destructor sends a quit down the dead socket and closes it), every handle on that server becomes "lost" (12500 on any
* use; CLOSE of it still works and frees the entry), and the call answers 12502.  THERE IS NO RETRY AND NO RECONNECT INSIDE AN OPERATION.
* The next NF$OPEN of that server dials again (a lost slot has been freed); an NF$OPEN on a connection that died without being noticed
* fails once with 12502 and the one after it reconnects.  Timeouts: NETWAIT seconds to connect (the class, connect.keys; 5 unless sd.conf says otherwise), then NETREPLY seconds for each
* read of a reply (30 unless sd.conf says otherwise; set with settimeout()).  Both come from the configuration since 19 Oct 26.  A server that is merely slow past that is treated as lost.
*
* WHAT THE CLASS DOES NOT DO, AND SO THIS PROGRAM DOES NOT: the class has no READV and no FILEINFO method.  NF$READV reads the whole
* record and cuts the field out here.  NF$FILEINFO answers keys 0 (1), 1 (null, no VOC name) and 2 (null: a path on another machine is not
* shown) without the server and refuses every other key (12507); a method on the class would let the rest through.  The class aborts the
* calling program when the server answers a request with an ON ERROR reply (for READ, a lock wait that gave up, a corrupt record ...), and
* that abort cannot be caught here.  So since slice 3e the object is made QUIET (obj->quiet, set in connect.new): the class then keeps the
* kind of the reply in obj->LastError (0 done, 1 ON ERROR, 2 ELSE, 3 error, 4 locked, -1 no reply read) and does not abort.  The text of an
* ON ERROR reply (obj->LastText, the payload of THIS reply) is shown, cleaned, by on.error.text, and a reply with no text shows 'status <n>' (19 Oct 26).
*
* rc<1>: 0 done; 2 the ELSE branch (the file, record or id was not there; rc<6> has the remote STATUS()); 3 an error; 4 (NF$LOCK only) the
* record is held by another user, rc<6> is its holder.  rc<2> is the message number, rc<3> to rc<5> its arguments.  rc<6> is a SIGNED whole
* number.  rc<7> is 1 on a good NF$OPEN of a write-mode handle.  EVERY ERROR MESSAGE NAMES THE SERVER, except 12499 (a handle that names no server) and, for the
* gate, 12465 (the server is then rc<4>, which the text does not print).
*
* END-DESCRIPTION
*
* START-CODE

$internal
subroutine netfile(op, a, b, c, out, rc)
$catalog $netfile

$include int$keys.h
$include keys.h
$include err.h
$include netfile.h

$define NF$NOREPLY    -2000000001 ;* ServerStatus before a request: still this afterwards means no reply came (a value no STATUS() takes: a changing request can be answered with -3023 and the like)
*  19 Oct 26 (K8d): the wait for each read of a reply is the configuration parameter NETREPLY (30 seconds by default; the plan, 4.6), read by request.limits - it was NF$REQUEST.MS, 30000.
$define NF$LIST       10          ;* the select list number used on the server (0 to 10 may be used by a client)
$define NF$CLEAR.MIN.MS 300000    ;* milliseconds to wait for the reply to a CLEARFILE at the least (a big file takes longer than 30 seconds; a false "lost" would drop the connection with the outcome unknown); a longer NETREPLY wins
$define NF$MAX.PACKET 1073741832  ;* the longest reply the class reads (obj->maxpacket, K8c): the largest string SD holds (MAX_STRING_SIZE, 1073741822) and the ten byte header.  A server that
                                  ;* announces more, or less than a header, cannot be telling the truth, and the call is treated as a lost connection without reading its body
$define NF$LE.OK      0           ;* obj->LastError: the kinds of reply (the server's SV$ codes); -1 is no reply read
$define NF$LE.ERROR   1           ;*   the server took its ON ERROR clause
$define NF$LE.LOCKED  4           ;*   the server took its LOCKED clause

*  The block is made by the first call and lives for the process.  COMMON STARTS AS 0, NOT UNASSIGNED.  Nothing secret is kept in it
*  (no key, no hash, no password); what the object itself holds is the class's business.
common /$netfile/ nf.opens,                     ;* remote files opened, in all
                  nf.requests,                  ;* requests sent after a login
                  nf.lost,                      ;* connections dropped: lost, or not held any more
                  nf.s.state(NF$MAX.SERVERS),   ;* 0 free, 1 connected
                  nf.s.name(NF$MAX.SERVERS),    ;* the server's name
                  nf.s.obj(NF$MAX.SERVERS),     ;* the !sdclient object (VARTYPE 15)
                  nf.h.state(NF$MAX.HANDLES),   ;* 0 free, 1 open, 2 lost with its connection
                  nf.h.slot(NF$MAX.HANDLES),    ;* the server's slot
                  nf.h.fno(NF$MAX.HANDLES),     ;* the file number the server gave
                  nf.h.srv(NF$MAX.HANDLES),     ;* the server's name (kept after the connection is gone)
                  nf.h.file(NF$MAX.HANDLES),    ;* the remote file name
                  nf.h.acct(NF$MAX.HANDLES),    ;* the account that opened it
                  nf.h.mode(NF$MAX.HANDLES),    ;* write or read: the mode the server's definition had at the open (slice 3e)
                  nf.h.aud(NF$MAX.HANDLES)      ;* letters of the audit lines already written for the handle: w write, d delete, r refused

*  Assigned first for BCOMP: an "is not assigned a value" warning fails the bootstrap.
   out = ''
   rc = ''
   rc<1> = NF$RC.OK
   failed = @false
   acc = downcase(@who)
   is.admin = @false
   gate.ok = @false
   srv = ''
   srvname = ''
   fname = ''
   body = ''
   is.dict = @false
   name.ok = @false
   fname.ok = @false
   safe = ''
   refused.why = ''
   defs = ''
   dx = 0
   n = 0
   found = 0
   host = ''
   port = ''
   racct = ''
   pin = ''
   accts = ''
   bad.field = ''
   cred = ''
   ruser = ''
   rsalt = ''
   riter = ''
   rck = ''
   rsk = ''
   err = ''
   cn = 0
   slot = 0
   sx = 0
   hx = 0
   hy = 0
   nh = 0
   ns = 0
   created = @false
   obj = 0
   ok = @false
   fno = 0
   e = ''
   id = ''
   fld = 0
   fkey = 0
   rec = ''
   lst = ''
   lk = 0
   st = 0
   rc.st = 0
   rq.lost = @false
   rq.err = @false
   rq.st = 0
   rq.txt = ''
   req.ms = 0
   clear.ms = 0
   opname = ''
   need.gate = @false
   need.conn = @false
   any.acct = @false
   allow.lost = @false
   fail.num = 0
   fail.a1 = ''
   fail.a2 = ''
   fail.a3 = ''
   txt = ''
   out.txt = ''
   chk.text = ''
   chk.set = ''
   chk.ok = @true
   ch = 0
   k = 0
   lc.set = 'abcdefghijklmnopqrstuvwxyz'    ;* not "lower": that is a BASIC function
   digits = '0123456789'
   hex.set = digits:'abcdef'
   marks = char(251):char(252):char(253):char(254):char(255)
   fmode = 'read'
   mode.ok = @false
   changing = @false
   upd = @false
   lerr = 0
   release.all = @false
   aud.k = ''
   aud.line = ''
   opn = 0
   res = ''

   begin case
      case op = NF$OPEN          ; gosub act.open
      case op = NF$CLOSE         ; gosub act.close
      case op = NF$READ          ; gosub act.read
      case op = NF$READV         ; gosub act.read
      case op = NF$RECORDLOCKED  ; gosub act.recordlocked
      case op = NF$SELECT        ; gosub act.select
      case op = NF$FILEINFO      ; gosub act.fileinfo
      case op = NF$CLOSEALL      ; gosub act.closeall
      case op = NF$WRITE or op = NF$WRITEU or op = NF$DELETE or op = NF$DELETEU or op = NF$CLEARFILE or op = NF$LOCK or op = NF$FILELOCK
         gosub act.change
      case op = NF$RELEASE       ; gosub act.release
      case op = NF$FILEUNLOCK    ; gosub act.fileunlock
      case op = NF$ADMIN         ; gosub act.admin
      case op = NF$CREATEFILE    ; gosub act.createfile
      case op = NF$FCONTROL      ; gosub act.fcontrol
      case op = NF$SEQKEY        ; gosub act.seqkey
      case op = NF$INDEXQ        ; gosub act.indexq
      case op = NF$STATE         ; gosub act.state
      case 1
         txt = op ; gosub clean.text
         fail.num = 12495 ; fail.a1 = out.txt
         gosub fail
   end case

   return

* ======================================================================
* act.open - NF$OPEN: a = server name, b = remote file name.  The seven checks, the audit line, then the connection and the file.
act.open:
   gosub shape.names
   safe = 'invalid'
   if name.ok then safe = srv
   srvname = safe

*  (1) to (6): the checks that need no network (the gate, the server, this account listed, the mode, the stored login, the limits).  21 Oct 26, S.73 item 3: they are check.server now, which
*  NF$CREATEFILE asks too; the file name (7) is this operation's alone.
   gosub check.server
   if failed then return

*  (7) THE REMOTE FILE NAME
   if not(fname.ok) then
      cred = ''
      txt = b ; gosub clean.text
      fail.num = 12496 ; fail.a1 = srv ; fail.a2 = out.txt
      refused.why = 'file' ; gosub refuse
      return
   end

*  Everything local has been asked.  One line in the audit trail for the open, and only now anything is dialled.
   if fmode = 'write' then
      void kernel(K$AUDIT, 'netfile open server=' : srv : ' file=' : fname : ' mode=write')
   end else
      void kernel(K$AUDIT, 'netfile open server=' : srv : ' file=' : fname)
   end

   created = @false
   if slot > 0 then
      cred = ''
      if vartype(nf.s.obj(slot)) # V$OBJ then
         gosub drop.conn
         fail.num = 12504 ; fail.a1 = srv
         gosub fail
         return
      end
   end else
      gosub connect.new
      if failed then return
      created = @true
   end

*  The file.  The object is taken out of common into obj; obj->error is cleared and ServerStatus set to the "no reply" mark first.
   obj = nf.s.obj(slot)
   obj->error = ''
   obj->ServerStatus = NF$NOREPLY
   fno = obj->open(fname)
   opname = 'open'
   gosub after.rq
   nf.requests += 1
   if rq.lost then
      gosub lost.fail
      return
   end
   if rq.err then
      gosub idle.release
      gosub server.fail
      return
   end
   if fno <= 0 then
*     The ELSE branch: the server has no such file (STATUS() 3003 as a rule).  A connection made for this call and used for nothing is ended.
      gosub idle.release
      rc.st = rq.st
      gosub rc.else
      return
   end

   hx = 0
   for hy = 1 to NF$MAX.HANDLES
      if nf.h.state(hy) = 0 and hx = 0 then hx = hy
   next hy
   if hx = 0 then
*     Not reachable after check 6, but the table is not trusted: give the file back.
      obj->error = ''
      obj->ServerStatus = NF$NOREPLY
      obj->close(fno)
      gosub idle.release
      fail.num = 12510 ; fail.a1 = srv ; fail.a2 = NF$MAX.HANDLES
      gosub fail
      return
   end
   nf.h.state(hx) = 1
   nf.h.slot(hx) = slot
   nf.h.fno(hx) = fno
   nf.h.srv(hx) = srv
   nf.h.file(hx) = fname
   nf.h.acct(hx) = acc
   nf.h.mode(hx) = fmode
   nf.h.aud(hx) = ''
   nf.opens += 1
   out = hx
   if fmode = 'write' then rc<7> = 1
   return

* ======================================================================
* check.server - the seven checks of NF$OPEN that need no network, in their order, for the server named in a (srv, srvname and safe are set by the caller after shape.names): (1) the gate,
* (2) a server that is defined and whose definition can be used, (3) this account is listed for it, (4) the mode (fmode: not a refusal), (5) the stored login (cred), (6) the limits.
* Sets failed, with rc and the audit line of a refusal, when one fails; otherwise host, port, racct, pin, fmode, cred and slot (the slot of a connection already held, or 0) are set.
* 21 Oct 26, S.73 item 3: this was the first part of act.open, moved out unchanged so that NF$CREATEFILE asks the same questions.
check.server:
*  (1) THE GATE
   gosub check.gate
   if not(gate.ok) then
      fail.num = 12465 ; fail.a1 = acc ; fail.a2 = safe
      refused.why = 'gate' ; gosub refuse
      return
   end

*  (2) THE SERVER: a well formed name, defined, and a definition that can be used
   if not(name.ok) then
      txt = a ; gosub clean.text
      fail.num = 12457 ; fail.a1 = out.txt
      refused.why = 'server' ; gosub refuse
      return
   end
   call !netdefs('READ', defs, err)
   if err # '' then
      fail.num = err<1,1> ; fail.a1 = err<1,2> ; fail.a2 = err<1,3> ; fail.a3 = err<1,4>
      refused.why = 'server' ; gosub refuse
      return
   end
   found = 0
   n = dcount(defs, @fm)
   for dx = 1 to n
      if dcount(defs<dx>, @vm) = 8 and defs<dx,1> = srv then found = dx
   next dx
   if found = 0 then
      fail.num = 12435 ; fail.a1 = srv
      refused.why = 'server' ; gosub refuse
      return
   end
   host = defs<found,2>
   port = defs<found,3>
   racct = defs<found,4>
   pin = downcase(defs<found,5>)
   accts = defs<found,7>
   gosub check.def
   if bad.field # '' then
      fail.num = 12497 ; fail.a1 = srv ; fail.a2 = bad.field
      refused.why = 'server' ; gosub refuse
      return
   end

*  (3) THIS ACCOUNT IS LISTED FOR THE SERVER (case blind; the administrator is exempt, as in set.netlogin)
   if not(is.admin) then
      if index(',':downcase(accts):',', ',':acc:',', 1) = 0 then
         fail.num = 12466 ; fail.a1 = acc ; fail.a2 = srv
         refused.why = 'list' ; gosub refuse
         return
      end
   end

*  (4) THE MODE: not a refusal.  The definition says write exactly, or the handle is read (anything else fails closed).  It is kept for the
*      handle and told to the kernel in rc<7>; every change asks the definition again (mode.now).
   fmode = 'read'
   if defs<found,6> = 'write' then fmode = 'write'

*  (5) THE STORED LOGIN, made for the key the definition holds now.  The remote user is attribute 1 of the credential, the account to enter
*      on the remote server is the definition's.
   cred = ''
   err = ''
   call $netcred('READ', srv, cred, pin, err)
   if err # '' then
      cred = ''
      cn = err<1,1>
      if cn = 12463 or cn = 12464 then
*        These two do not name the server: say it, with the text of the netcred message.
         txt = sysmsg(cn, err<1,2>) ; gosub clean.text
         fail.num = 12501 ; fail.a1 = srv ; fail.a2 = out.txt
      end else
         fail.num = cn ; fail.a1 = err<1,2> ; fail.a2 = err<1,3> ; fail.a3 = err<1,4>
      end
      refused.why = 'credential' ; gosub refuse
      return
   end

*  (6) THE LIMITS
   gosub find.slot
   gosub count.up
   if slot = 0 and ns >= NF$MAX.SERVERS then
      cred = ''
      fail.num = 12498 ; fail.a1 = NF$MAX.SERVERS ; fail.a2 = srv
      refused.why = 'limit' ; gosub refuse
      return
   end
   if nh >= NF$MAX.HANDLES then
      cred = ''
      fail.num = 12510 ; fail.a1 = srv ; fail.a2 = nh
      refused.why = 'limit' ; gosub refuse
      return
   end
   return

* ======================================================================
* connect.new - a connection to srv in a free slot (host, port, racct, pin and cred are set).  Sets slot, or failed with rc.
connect.new:
   slot = 0
   for sx = 1 to NF$MAX.SERVERS
      if nf.s.state(sx) = 0 and slot = 0 then slot = sx
   next sx
   if slot = 0 then
      cred = ''
      fail.num = 12498 ; fail.a1 = NF$MAX.SERVERS ; fail.a2 = srv
      gosub fail
      return
   end

*  The keys are copied out of the credential, the credential is cleared, and each copy is cleared the moment the login has used it.
   ruser = cred<1>
   rsalt = cred<2>
   riter = cred<3>
   rck = cred<4>
   rsk = cred<5>
   cred = ''
   obj = object('!sdclient')
   ok = obj->connect.keys(host, port, ruser, rsalt, riter, rck, rsk, racct, pin)
   ruser = ''
   rsalt = ''
   riter = ''
   rck = ''
   rsk = ''
   if not(ok) then
      txt = obj->error ; gosub clean.text
      obj = 0
      slot = 0
      fail.num = 12503 ; fail.a1 = srv ; fail.a2 = out.txt
      gosub fail
      return
   end
*  QUIET: a reply of the ON ERROR kind must not abort this program (an abort cannot be caught here); obj->LastError says what the reply was.
   obj->quiet = @true
   obj->maxpacket = NF$MAX.PACKET
   gosub request.limits
   obj->settimeout(req.ms)

*  THE OBJECT GOES INTO COMMON.  It is looked at at once: if the block did not keep an object, nothing is left half done.
   nf.s.obj(slot) = obj
   if vartype(nf.s.obj(slot)) # V$OBJ then
      obj->disconnect
      obj = 0
      nf.s.obj(slot) = 0
      slot = 0
      fail.num = 12504 ; fail.a1 = srv
      gosub fail
      return
   end
   nf.s.state(slot) = 1
   nf.s.name(slot) = srv
   return

* ======================================================================
* act.close - NF$CLOSE: a = handle.  A handle of a lost connection is freed without a word; a close the server did not answer frees it too.
act.close:
   need.gate = @false ; need.conn = @false ; any.acct = @true ; allow.lost = @true
   gosub get.handle
   if failed then return
   if nf.h.state(hx) = 1 then
      if vartype(nf.s.obj(slot)) = V$OBJ then
         obj = nf.s.obj(slot)
         obj->error = ''
         obj->ServerStatus = NF$NOREPLY
         obj->close(nf.h.fno(hx))
         opname = 'close'
         gosub after.rq
         nf.requests += 1
         if rq.lost then gosub drop.conn
         obj = 0
      end
   end
   gosub free.handle
   return

* ======================================================================
* act.read - NF$READ and NF$READV: a = handle, b = record id, c = field number (READV)
act.read:
   need.gate = @true ; need.conn = @true ; any.acct = @false ; allow.lost = @false
   gosub get.handle
   if failed then return
   gosub check.id
   if failed then return
   if op = NF$READV then
      opname = 'readv'
      gosub check.field
      if failed then return
   end else
      opname = 'read'
   end
   obj = nf.s.obj(slot)
   obj->error = ''
   obj->ServerStatus = NF$NOREPLY
   e = ''
   rec = obj->read(nf.h.fno(hx), id, e)
   gosub after.rq
   nf.requests += 1
   if rq.lost then
      gosub lost.fail
      return
   end
   if rq.err then
      gosub server.fail
      return
   end
   begin case
      case e = 0
         if op = NF$READV then
            if fld > 0 then out = rec<fld>
         end else
            out = rec
         end
      case e = 2
         rc.st = rq.st
         gosub rc.else
      case e = 1
*        The server took its ON ERROR clause or refused the request (the object is quiet, so nothing was aborted): its text, or the status when it sent none.
         gosub on.error.text
         gosub server.fail
      case 1
         fail.num = 12509 ; fail.a1 = srvname ; fail.a2 = opname ; fail.a3 = 'answer ':e
         gosub fail
   end case
   return

* ======================================================================
* act.recordlocked - NF$RECORDLOCKED: a = handle, b = record id.  out is the class's code (0 not locked).
* S.72b: only a write-mode handle may ask (the kernel refuses a read-mode one first, with the same message 12506; this is the same rule one layer down, asked before the
* connection is, like act.fileunlock).  A transaction does not matter: it changes nothing.  The server's wall is request 34 in the change set of !apifilesok.
act.recordlocked:
   need.gate = @false ; need.conn = @false ; any.acct = @false ; allow.lost = @true
   gosub get.handle
   if failed then return
   opname = 'recordlocked'
   if nf.h.mode(hx) # 'write' then
      gosub act.refuse
      return
   end
   need.gate = @true ; need.conn = @true ; any.acct = @false ; allow.lost = @false
   gosub get.handle
   if failed then return
   gosub check.id
   if failed then return
   obj = nf.s.obj(slot)
   obj->error = ''
   obj->ServerStatus = NF$NOREPLY
   lk = obj->recordlocked(nf.h.fno(hx), id)
   lerr = obj->LastError
   gosub after.rq
   nf.requests += 1
   if lerr = -1 then rq.lost = @true
   if rq.lost then
      gosub lost.fail
      return
   end
   if rq.err then
      gosub server.fail
      return
   end
*  S.72b: the server REFUSED the request (a login without sdfileswrite: message 12526 as an ON ERROR reply); the class is quiet, so it returned 0 and said what the reply was.
   if lerr = NF$LE.ERROR then
      gosub on.error.text
      gosub server.fail
      return
   end
   if lerr # NF$LE.OK then
      fail.num = 12509 ; fail.a1 = srvname ; fail.a2 = opname ; fail.a3 = 'answer ':lerr
      gosub fail
      return
   end
   out = lk
*  S.72: the server leaves the user number of the lock's owner in STATUS() (apisrvr: set.status after recordlocked()); the kernel gives it to a RECORDLOCKED() as the status
   rc<8> = rq.st
   return

* ======================================================================
* act.select - NF$SELECT: a = handle.  Two requests: SELECT into list NF$LIST, then READLIST, which returns every id and uses the list up.
act.select:
   need.gate = @true ; need.conn = @true ; any.acct = @false ; allow.lost = @false
   gosub get.handle
   if failed then return
   opname = 'select'
   obj = nf.s.obj(slot)
   obj->error = ''
   obj->ServerStatus = NF$NOREPLY
   obj->select(nf.h.fno(hx), NF$LIST)
   gosub after.rq
   nf.requests += 1
   if rq.lost then
      gosub lost.fail
      return
   end
   if rq.err then
      gosub server.fail
      return
   end
*  19 Oct 26 (verifier, finish 2): the SELECT proper answered with an ON ERROR is the server's refusal too.  The class used to ABORT on it even when quiet, so this branch could not run;
*  select() honours quiet now and says what the reply was in obj->LastError.
   if obj->LastError = NF$LE.ERROR then
      gosub on.error.text
      gosub server.fail
      return
   end
   obj->error = ''
   obj->ServerStatus = NF$NOREPLY
   e = ''
   lst = obj->readlist(NF$LIST, e)
   gosub after.rq
   nf.requests += 1
   if rq.lost then
      gosub lost.fail
      return
   end
   if rq.err then
      gosub server.fail
      return
   end
   if e = 1 then
      gosub on.error.text
      gosub server.fail
      return
   end
   if e # 0 then
      fail.num = 12509 ; fail.a1 = srvname ; fail.a2 = opname ; fail.a3 = 'answer ':e
      gosub fail
      return
   end
   out = lst
   return

* ======================================================================
* act.fileinfo - NF$FILEINFO: a = handle, b = key.  Answered here for FL$OPEN, FL$VOCNAME and FL$PATH; any other key is refused (12507).
act.fileinfo:
   need.gate = @true ; need.conn = @false ; any.acct = @false ; allow.lost = @false
   gosub get.handle
   if failed then return
   opname = 'fileinfo'
   fkey = -1
   if b matches '1N0N' then
      if len(b) <= 6 then fkey = b + 0
   end
   begin case
      case fkey = FL$OPEN    ; out = 1
      case fkey = FL$VOCNAME ; out = ''
      case fkey = FL$PATH    ; out = ''
      case 1
         txt = b ; gosub clean.text
         fail.num = 12507 ; fail.a1 = srvname ; fail.a2 = out.txt
         gosub fail
   end case
   return

* ======================================================================
* act.closeall - NF$CLOSEALL: end every connection of the process and forget every handle.  Always done, never refused, sends nothing but a quit
* on each connection that is still held.  For LOGTO and for the end of a session.
act.closeall:
   for sx = 1 to NF$MAX.SERVERS
      if vartype(nf.s.obj(sx)) = V$OBJ then
         obj = nf.s.obj(sx)
         obj->disconnect
         obj = 0
      end
      nf.s.obj(sx) = 0
      nf.s.state(sx) = 0
      nf.s.name(sx) = ''
   next sx
   for hy = 1 to NF$MAX.HANDLES
      gosub clear.handle
   next hy
   return

* ======================================================================
* act.change - NF$WRITE, NF$WRITEU, NF$DELETE, NF$DELETEU, NF$CLEARFILE and NF$LOCK.  a = handle, b = record id, c = the record (a write) or the
* lock kind l / u (a lock).  THE ORDER OF THE CHECKS IS PART OF THE DESIGN:
*     1  a handle of this account (a lost handle will do)  2  the operation's name  3  the handle's mode is not write: refused (12506), as
*     before slice 3e  4  the handle again, with the gate and the connection (a lost handle: 12500)  5  the definition says write NOW
*     6  no transaction is open (12515)  7  the id and the lock kind  8  the audit line  9  the request, always with the "no reply" mark
*     10 the outcome.  A changing request is NEVER retried.  A lock is only ever asked for without waiting: the kernel waits, by asking again.
act.change:
   need.gate = @false ; need.conn = @false ; any.acct = @false ; allow.lost = @true
   gosub get.handle
   if failed then return
   begin case
      case op = NF$WRITE     ; opname = 'write'
      case op = NF$WRITEU    ; opname = 'writeu'
      case op = NF$DELETE    ; opname = 'delete'
      case op = NF$DELETEU   ; opname = 'deleteu'
      case op = NF$CLEARFILE ; opname = 'clear.file'
      case op = NF$FILELOCK  ; opname = 'filelock'
      case 1                 ; opname = 'lock'
   end case
   changing = (op # NF$LOCK and op # NF$FILELOCK)

*  A read-mode handle: the refusal of slice 3c, byte for byte, and nothing is sent.
   if nf.h.mode(hx) # 'write' then
      gosub act.refuse
      return
   end

   need.gate = @true ; need.conn = @true ; any.acct = @false ; allow.lost = @false
   gosub get.handle
   if failed then return

*  The definition is read again: a server set back to read stops writes on handles that are already open.
   gosub mode.now
   if not(mode.ok) then
      aud.k = 'r'
      aud.line = 'netfile refused server=' : srvname : ' reason=mode'
      gosub audit.once
      gosub act.refuse
      return
   end

*  A change on a remote file never joins a local transaction, so it is refused inside one (the kernel refuses first; this is for a direct caller).
   if system(SYS$TRANS_LEVEL) # 0 then
      fail.num = 12515 ; fail.a1 = srvname ; fail.a2 = opname
      gosub fail
      return
   end

   if op # NF$CLEARFILE and op # NF$FILELOCK then
      gosub check.id.chg
      if failed then return
   end
   if op = NF$LOCK then
      if c # 'l' and c # 'u' then
         txt = c ; gosub clean.text
         fail.num = 12517 ; fail.a1 = srvname ; fail.a2 = out.txt
         gosub fail
         return
      end
      upd = (c = 'u')
   end

*  The audit line, before the request.  No id and no record data in it.  A lock is not audited.
   begin case
      case op = NF$WRITE or op = NF$WRITEU
         aud.k = 'w'
         aud.line = 'netfile write server=' : srvname : ' file=' : nf.h.file(hx)
         gosub audit.once
      case op = NF$DELETE or op = NF$DELETEU
         aud.k = 'd'
         aud.line = 'netfile delete server=' : srvname : ' file=' : nf.h.file(hx)
         gosub audit.once
      case op = NF$CLEARFILE
         void kernel(K$AUDIT, 'netfile clear server=' : srvname : ' file=' : nf.h.file(hx))
      case op = NF$FILELOCK
*        S.72: a file lock held against everyone is audited, once for the handle (the wait is many requests); no id is involved
         aud.k = 'f'
         aud.line = 'netfile filelock server=' : srvname : ' file=' : nf.h.file(hx)
         gosub audit.once
   end case

*  The request.  The no-reply mark comes right before each one.
   obj = nf.s.obj(slot)
   begin case
      case op = NF$WRITE
         obj->error = ''
         obj->ServerStatus = NF$NOREPLY
         obj->write(nf.h.fno(hx), id, c)
      case op = NF$WRITEU
         obj->error = ''
         obj->ServerStatus = NF$NOREPLY
         obj->writeu(nf.h.fno(hx), id, c)
      case op = NF$DELETE
         obj->error = ''
         obj->ServerStatus = NF$NOREPLY
         obj->delete(nf.h.fno(hx), id)
      case op = NF$DELETEU
         obj->error = ''
         obj->ServerStatus = NF$NOREPLY
         obj->deleteu(nf.h.fno(hx), id)
      case op = NF$CLEARFILE
         gosub request.limits
         obj->settimeout(clear.ms)
         obj->error = ''
         obj->ServerStatus = NF$NOREPLY
         obj->clearfile(nf.h.fno(hx))
      case op = NF$FILELOCK
*        S.72: the wait flag is the literal @false - the server is never made to block; a busy file is asked for again by the kernel
         obj->error = ''
         obj->ServerStatus = NF$NOREPLY
         obj->filelock(nf.h.fno(hx), @false)
      case 1
         obj->error = ''
         obj->ServerStatus = NF$NOREPLY
         obj->recordlock(nf.h.fno(hx), id, upd, @false)
   end case
   lerr = obj->LastError
   gosub after.rq
   nf.requests += 1
   if lerr = -1 then rq.lost = @true
   if op = NF$CLEARFILE then
      if not(rq.lost) then obj->settimeout(req.ms)
   end

*  The outcome.
   if rq.lost then
      gosub lost.fail
      return
   end
   if rq.err then
      gosub server.fail
      return
   end
   if lerr = NF$LE.OK then return
   if (op = NF$LOCK or op = NF$FILELOCK) and lerr = NF$LE.LOCKED then
      gosub rc.locked
      return
   end
   if lerr = NF$LE.ERROR then
*     The server took its ON ERROR clause or refused the request (the files-only login without the write group: 12526): its text, or the status when it sent none.
      gosub on.error.text
      gosub server.fail
      return
   end
   fail.num = 12509 ; fail.a1 = srvname ; fail.a2 = opname ; fail.a3 = 'answer ':lerr
   gosub fail
   return

* ======================================================================
* act.refuse - the refusal of a change or lock on a read-mode handle (or one whose definition says read now): 12506, naming the server and the
* operation in opname.  Nothing is sent.
act.refuse:
   fail.num = 12506 ; fail.a1 = srvname ; fail.a2 = opname
   gosub fail
   return

* ======================================================================
* act.release - NF$RELEASE: a = handle, or 0 for every lock of the session on every connected server that has a write-mode handle open;
* b = record id (null: every lock on the file).  Never refused for the mode or a transaction (releasing changes no data) and it never fails
* because the server or the connection did: a lost connection has lost its locks with it.  Only a handle that is not this account's, or an id
* over MAX.ID.LEN, is an error.  Not audited.
act.release:
   need.gate = @false ; need.conn = @false ; any.acct = @false ; allow.lost = @true
   opname = 'release'
   release.all = @false
   if a matches '1N0N' then
      if len(a) <= 9 then release.all = (a + 0 = 0)
   end
   if release.all then
      gosub release.every
      return
   end
   gosub get.handle
   if failed then return
   if nf.h.state(hx) # 1 then return
   id = b
   if len(id) > MAX.ID.LEN then
      fail.num = 12508 ; fail.a1 = srvname ; fail.a2 = 'too long'
      gosub fail
      return
   end
   if vartype(nf.s.obj(slot)) # V$OBJ then
      gosub drop.conn
      return
   end
   obj = nf.s.obj(slot)
   obj->error = ''
   obj->ServerStatus = NF$NOREPLY
   obj->release(nf.h.fno(hx), id)
   gosub after.rq
   nf.requests += 1
   if rq.lost then gosub drop.conn
   obj = 0
   return

* ======================================================================
* act.fileunlock - NF$FILEUNLOCK (S.72): a = handle.  Gives the file lock back.  THE ORDER: a handle of this account (a lost handle will do), a handle whose mode is not write is
* refused (12506, nothing sent - it can hold no file lock), then the gate and the connection, no transaction (12515), and the request.  UNLIKE act.change THE DEFINITION IS NOT
* READ AGAIN: a lock that is held must always be able to be given back, and giving it back changes no data (act.release is the same).  Not audited.  The answer is the reply STATUS()
* of request 33 in rc<8>: 0 the lock was this session's and is free, 3011 (ER$LCK) another user's, still held, 3012 (ER$NLK) nobody's.  A lost reply is 12502 (a lock changes no data:
* not 12514) and the connection is dropped; nothing is sent twice.
act.fileunlock:
   need.gate = @false ; need.conn = @false ; any.acct = @false ; allow.lost = @true
   gosub get.handle
   if failed then return
   opname = 'fileunlock'
   if nf.h.mode(hx) # 'write' then
      gosub act.refuse
      return
   end
   need.gate = @true ; need.conn = @true ; any.acct = @false ; allow.lost = @false
   gosub get.handle
   if failed then return
   if system(SYS$TRANS_LEVEL) # 0 then
      fail.num = 12515 ; fail.a1 = srvname ; fail.a2 = opname
      gosub fail
      return
   end
   obj = nf.s.obj(slot)
   obj->error = ''
   obj->ServerStatus = NF$NOREPLY
   obj->fileunlock(nf.h.fno(hx))
   lerr = obj->LastError
   gosub after.rq
   nf.requests += 1
   if lerr = -1 then rq.lost = @true
   if rq.lost then
      gosub lost.fail
      return
   end
   if rq.err then
      gosub server.fail
      return
   end
   if lerr = NF$LE.ERROR then
      gosub on.error.text
      gosub server.fail
      return
   end
   if lerr # NF$LE.OK then
      fail.num = 12509 ; fail.a1 = srvname ; fail.a2 = opname ; fail.a3 = 'answer ':lerr
      gosub fail
      return
   end
   rc<8> = rq.st
   return

* ======================================================================
* S.73 item 3 (21 Oct 26), QMNet tiers C and D: the four operations that ADMINISTER a remote file.  Each needs a handle whose definition says write (now, not only at the open), no transaction
* (but ANALYSE.FILE, which only reads), and - on the server - the groups sdfileswrite and sdfilesadmin: the server's refusal (12526 or 12529, an ON ERROR reply) is the one wall; everything
* here is the clear text of it.  A change is never repeated: a lost reply is 12514 and the connection is dropped.  A verb can run for a long time (an index on a big file), so the wait for
* the reply is never less than NF$CLEAR.MIN.MS, as for CLEAR.FILE.

* admin.prepare - the checks the operations share, in the order of act.change: (1) a handle of this account (a lost handle will do), (2) the handle's mode is not write: refused (12506), nothing is
* sent, (3) the handle again with the gate and the connection, (4) the definition says write NOW (mode.now), (5) no transaction is open when the operation changes the file (changing: 12515).
* opname and changing are set by the caller.  Sets failed (with rc) when the operation may not go on; otherwise hx, slot and srvname are set.
admin.prepare:
   need.gate = @false ; need.conn = @false ; any.acct = @false ; allow.lost = @true
   gosub get.handle
   if failed then return
   if nf.h.mode(hx) # 'write' then
      gosub act.refuse
      return
   end
   need.gate = @true ; need.conn = @true ; any.acct = @false ; allow.lost = @false
   gosub get.handle
   if failed then return
   gosub mode.now
   if not(mode.ok) then
      aud.k = 'r'
      aud.line = 'netfile refused server=' : srvname : ' reason=mode'
      gosub audit.once
      gosub act.refuse
      return
   end
   if changing then
      if system(SYS$TRANS_LEVEL) # 0 then
         fail.num = 12515 ; fail.a1 = srvname ; fail.a2 = opname
         gosub fail
         return
      end
   end
   return

* admin.outcome - the answer to the request just made on obj (the caller cleared obj->error, set the "no reply" mark and called the method): lost (the connection is dropped), an error of the server,
* the ON ERROR reply of a refusal (its text, or the status when it sent none), or done: rc<8> is the server's reply STATUS(), the verb's return code or what FCONTROL left.
admin.outcome:
   lerr = obj->LastError
   gosub after.rq
   nf.requests += 1
   if lerr = -1 then rq.lost = @true
   if rq.lost then
      gosub lost.fail
      return
   end
   if rq.err then
      gosub server.fail
      return
   end
   if lerr = NF$LE.ERROR then
      gosub on.error.text
      gosub server.fail
      return
   end
   if lerr # NF$LE.OK then
      fail.num = 12509 ; fail.a1 = srvname ; fail.a2 = opname ; fail.a3 = 'answer ':lerr
      gosub fail
      return
   end
   rc<8> = rq.st
   return

* act.admin - NF$ADMIN: a = handle, b = the operation (1 analyse.file, 2 configure.file, 3 create.index, 4 build.index, 5 delete.index, 6 disable.index, 9 delete.file), c = the verb's words.  out is what the
* verb printed.  The handle ends with a delete.file that was answered (the server closed the file to delete it).
act.admin:
   opn = -1
   if b matches '1N0N' then
      if len(b) <= 2 then opn = b + 0
   end
   opname = ''
   if opn >= 1 and opn <= 9 and opn # 7 and opn # 8 then
      opname = field('analyse.file,configure.file,create.index,build.index,delete.index,disable.index,,create.file,delete.file', ',', opn)
   end
   if opname = '' then
      txt = b ; gosub clean.text
      fail.num = 12495 ; fail.a1 = out.txt
      gosub fail
      return
   end
   changing = (opn # 1)
   gosub admin.prepare
   if failed then return

   void kernel(K$AUDIT, 'netfile admin server=' : srvname : ' file=' : nf.h.file(hx) : ' op=' : opname)
   obj = nf.s.obj(slot)
   gosub request.limits
   obj->settimeout(clear.ms)
   obj->error = ''
   obj->ServerStatus = NF$NOREPLY
   res = obj->fileadmin(opn, nf.h.fno(hx), c)
   gosub admin.outcome
   if not(rq.lost) then obj->settimeout(req.ms)
   if failed then return
   out = res
   if opn = 9 then gosub free.handle
   return

* act.createfile - NF$CREATEFILE: a = the server's name, c = the words of create.file, the new file's name first.  There is no file yet, so no handle: the checks of NF$OPEN that need no network are
* asked (check.server), the definition must say write (12506), no transaction (12515), then the connection (the one held, or a new one that is ended again afterwards) and request 70 operation 8.
* out is what the verb printed.  The caller (the kernel) writes the local pointer only after this returned.
act.createfile:
   b = ''
   gosub shape.names
   safe = 'invalid'
   if name.ok then safe = srv
   srvname = safe
   opname = 'create.file'
   changing = @true
   gosub check.server
   if failed then return
   if fmode # 'write' then
      cred = ''
      gosub act.refuse
      return
   end
   if system(SYS$TRANS_LEVEL) # 0 then
      cred = ''
      fail.num = 12515 ; fail.a1 = srvname ; fail.a2 = opname
      gosub fail
      return
   end

   txt = field(c, ' ', 1) ; gosub clean.text
   void kernel(K$AUDIT, 'netfile admin server=' : srv : ' file=' : out.txt : ' op=create.file')
   created = @false
   if slot > 0 then
      cred = ''
      if vartype(nf.s.obj(slot)) # V$OBJ then
         gosub drop.conn
         fail.num = 12504 ; fail.a1 = srv
         gosub fail
         return
      end
   end else
      gosub connect.new
      if failed then return
      created = @true
   end
   obj = nf.s.obj(slot)
   gosub request.limits
   obj->settimeout(clear.ms)
   obj->error = ''
   obj->ServerStatus = NF$NOREPLY
   res = obj->fileadmin(8, 0, c)
   gosub admin.outcome
   if not(rq.lost) then
      obj->settimeout(req.ms)
      gosub idle.release
   end
   if failed then return
   out = res
   return

* act.fcontrol - NF$FCONTROL: a = handle, b = the key, c = the qualifier as text (null for none).  out is what the server's FCONTROL() returned, rc<8> what it left in STATUS().  Key 6 (FC$VALID.ID) only
* asks and changes nothing; every other key is a change.
act.fcontrol:
   opname = 'fcontrol'
   fkey = -1
   if b matches '1N0N' then
      if len(b) <= 5 then fkey = b + 0
   end
   if fkey < 0 then
      txt = b ; gosub clean.text
      fail.num = 12495 ; fail.a1 = out.txt
      gosub fail
      return
   end
   changing = (fkey # 6)
   gosub admin.prepare
   if failed then return

   void kernel(K$AUDIT, 'netfile fcontrol server=' : srvname : ' file=' : nf.h.file(hx) : ' key=' : fkey)
   obj = nf.s.obj(slot)
   obj->error = ''
   obj->ServerStatus = NF$NOREPLY
   res = obj->fcontrol(nf.h.fno(hx), fkey, c)
   gosub admin.outcome
   if failed then return
   out = res
   return

* act.seqkey - NF$SEQKEY: a = handle, b = 0 for WRITE, 1 for WRITEU, c = the record.  out is the id the server's counter gave it (an update lock on it is held by this session on the server).  Audited as
* a write, once for the handle.
act.seqkey:
   upd = (b = '1')
   if upd then opname = 'writeu' else opname = 'write'
   changing = @true
   gosub admin.prepare
   if failed then return

   aud.k = 'w'
   aud.line = 'netfile write server=' : srvname : ' file=' : nf.h.file(hx)
   gosub audit.once
   obj = nf.s.obj(slot)
   if upd then
      obj->error = ''
      obj->ServerStatus = NF$NOREPLY
      res = obj->writeuseqkey(nf.h.fno(hx), c)
   end else
      obj->error = ''
      obj->ServerStatus = NF$NOREPLY
      res = obj->writeseqkey(nf.h.fno(hx), c)
   end
   gosub admin.outcome
   if failed then return
   if res = '' then
      fail.num = 12509 ; fail.a1 = srvname ; fail.a2 = opname ; fail.a3 = 'no id'
      gosub fail
      return
   end
   out = res
   return

* ======================================================================
* act.indexq - NF$INDEXQ: a = handle, b = what (1 to 8), c = its argument.  The index QUERY statements of a remote file (21 Oct 26, S.73 item 3, D1: the owner's "no new gate"): they change nothing, so a read-mode handle
* may ask, a transaction does not matter, they are not audited (a SELECT is not either), and the server serves them to every sdfiles login.  b: 1 INDICES(f), 2 INDICES(f, name) (c = name), 3 SELECTINDEX name FROM f -
* the indexed values (c = name), 4 SELECTINDEX name, value FROM f - the ids (c = name, a field mark, the value), 5 SETLEFT name, 6 SETRIGHT name, 7 SELECTLEFT name and 8 SELECTRIGHT name (c = name; two requests: the scan,
* which answers the key, and READLIST for the ids).  out is the answer; for 7 and 8 the key, a field mark and the ids.  rc<8> is the server's STATUS() of the statement: 0 done, 1 no such index (7 and 8: no further key,
* and then there is no second request).
act.indexq:
   need.gate = @true ; need.conn = @true ; any.acct = @false ; allow.lost = @false
   gosub get.handle
   if failed then return
   opname = 'index'
   what = 0
   if b matches '1N' then what = b + 0
   if what < 1 or what > 8 then
      txt = b ; gosub clean.text
      fail.num = 12495 ; fail.a1 = out.txt
      gosub fail
      return
   end
   iname = c ; ival = ''
   if what = 4 then
      fmp = index(c, @fm, 1)
      if fmp > 0 then
         iname = c[1, fmp - 1]
         ival = c[fmp + 1, len(c)]
      end
   end
   obj = nf.s.obj(slot)
   obj->error = ''
   res = ''
* each arm marks its own request (the mark and the request are on one line: NF7 reads them so)
   begin case
      case what = 1 ; obj->ServerStatus = NF$NOREPLY ; res = obj->indices(nf.h.fno(hx), '')
      case what = 2 ; obj->ServerStatus = NF$NOREPLY ; res = obj->indices(nf.h.fno(hx), iname)
      case what = 3 ; obj->ServerStatus = NF$NOREPLY ; res = obj->selectindexv(nf.h.fno(hx), iname)
      case what = 4 ; obj->ServerStatus = NF$NOREPLY ; res = obj->selectindexk(nf.h.fno(hx), iname, ival)
      case what = 5 ; obj->ServerStatus = NF$NOREPLY ; obj->setleft(nf.h.fno(hx), iname)
      case what = 6 ; obj->ServerStatus = NF$NOREPLY ; obj->setright(nf.h.fno(hx), iname)
      case what = 7 ; obj->ServerStatus = NF$NOREPLY ; res = obj->selectleft(nf.h.fno(hx), iname, NF$LIST)
      case what = 8 ; obj->ServerStatus = NF$NOREPLY ; res = obj->selectright(nf.h.fno(hx), iname, NF$LIST)
   end case
   gosub admin.outcome
   if failed then return
   out = res
   if what >= 7 and rc<8> = 0 then
      st1 = rc<8>
      key = res
      obj->error = ''
      obj->ServerStatus = NF$NOREPLY
      e = ''
      lst = obj->readlist(NF$LIST, e)
      gosub after.rq
      nf.requests += 1
      if rq.lost then
         gosub lost.fail
         return
      end
      if rq.err then
         gosub server.fail
         return
      end
      if e = 1 then
         gosub on.error.text
         gosub server.fail
         return
      end
      if e # 0 then
         fail.num = 12509 ; fail.a1 = srvname ; fail.a2 = opname ; fail.a3 = 'answer ':e
         gosub fail
         return
      end
      out = key : @fm : lst
      rc<8> = st1
   end
   return

* ======================================================================
* release.every - one release-all request on each connected server that has a write-mode handle open.  A lost reply drops that connection
* and goes on to the next.
release.every:
   for sx = 1 to NF$MAX.SERVERS
      if nf.s.state(sx) = 1 then
         n = 0
         for hy = 1 to NF$MAX.HANDLES
            if nf.h.state(hy) = 1 then
               if nf.h.slot(hy) = sx and nf.h.mode(hy) = 'write' then n += 1
            end
         next hy
         if n > 0 and vartype(nf.s.obj(sx)) = V$OBJ then
            obj = nf.s.obj(sx)
            obj->error = ''
            obj->ServerStatus = NF$NOREPLY
            obj->release(0, '')
            gosub after.rq
            nf.requests += 1
            if rq.lost then
               slot = sx
               gosub drop.conn
            end
            obj = 0
         end
      end
   next sx
   return

* ======================================================================
* act.state - NF$STATE: out<1> servers connected, <2> files open, <3> files opened in all, <4> requests sent, <5> connections dropped,
* <6> files lost with their connection; then one attribute for each connected server: name, VARTYPE of its object (15 is an object), files open.
act.state:
   gosub count.up
   out = ''
   out<1> = ns
   out<2> = nh
   out<3> = nf.opens
   out<4> = nf.requests
   out<5> = nf.lost
   n = 0
   for hy = 1 to NF$MAX.HANDLES
      if nf.h.state(hy) = 2 then n += 1
   next hy
   out<6> = n
   for sx = 1 to NF$MAX.SERVERS
      if nf.s.state(sx) = 1 then
         n = 0
         for hy = 1 to NF$MAX.HANDLES
            if nf.h.state(hy) = 1 then
               if nf.h.slot(hy) = sx then n += 1
            end
         next hy
         out<-1> = nf.s.name(sx) : @vm : vartype(nf.s.obj(sx)) : @vm : n
      end
   next sx
   return

* ======================================================================
* get.handle - the handle in a, for an operation on it.  The caller sets need.gate (ask the NET gate again), need.conn (the connection must be
* held), any.acct (a handle of another account will do) and allow.lost (a handle of a lost connection will do).  Sets hx and slot (and
* srvname); failed, with rc, when the handle is not usable.
get.handle:
   failed = @false
   hx = 0
   slot = 0
   if a matches '1N0N' then
      if len(a) <= 9 then hx = a + 0
   end
   if hx < 1 or hx > NF$MAX.HANDLES then
      hx = 0
      txt = a ; gosub clean.text
      fail.num = 12499 ; fail.a1 = out.txt
      gosub fail
      return
   end
   if nf.h.state(hx) = 0 then
      fail.num = 12499 ; fail.a1 = hx
      hx = 0
      gosub fail
      return
   end
   srvname = nf.h.srv(hx)
   if nf.h.state(hx) = 2 then
      if allow.lost then return
      fail.num = 12500 ; fail.a1 = srvname ; fail.a2 = hx
      gosub fail
      return
   end
   if need.gate then
      gosub check.gate
      if not(gate.ok) then
         fail.num = 12465 ; fail.a1 = acc ; fail.a2 = srvname
         gosub fail
         return
      end
   end
   if not(any.acct) then
      if nf.h.acct(hx) # acc then
         fail.num = 12499 ; fail.a1 = hx
         gosub fail
         return
      end
   end
   slot = nf.h.slot(hx)
   if slot < 1 or slot > NF$MAX.SERVERS then
*     A handle that says it is open on no server: lost.
      slot = 0
      nf.h.state(hx) = 2
      fail.num = 12500 ; fail.a1 = srvname ; fail.a2 = hx
      gosub fail
      return
   end
   if need.conn then
      if vartype(nf.s.obj(slot)) # V$OBJ then
         gosub drop.conn
         fail.num = 12504 ; fail.a1 = srvname
         gosub fail
         return
      end
   end
   return

* ======================================================================
* check.id - the record id in b becomes id.  Empty is the ELSE branch with STATUS() 3000 (as a local READ); an id longer than MAX.ID.LEN or
* holding a mark is an error.  Sets failed (and rc) in both cases, so the caller stops.
check.id:
   id = b
   if id = '' then
      rc.st = ER$IID
      gosub rc.else
      failed = @true
      return
   end
   gosub check.id.shape
   return

* ======================================================================
* check.id.chg - the record id in b becomes id, for a write, delete or lock.  A write or delete has no ELSE branch, so an EMPTY id is an error
* here (12508).  The kernel gives -ER$IID for an empty id before it calls, so this is the defence for a direct caller.
check.id.chg:
   id = b
   if id = '' then
      fail.num = 12508 ; fail.a1 = srvname ; fail.a2 = 'empty'
      gosub fail
      return
   end
   gosub check.id.shape
   return

* ======================================================================
* check.id.shape - the id in id (not empty) is not longer than MAX.ID.LEN and holds no mark; failed, with rc, when it does.
check.id.shape:
   if len(id) > MAX.ID.LEN then
      fail.num = 12508 ; fail.a1 = srvname ; fail.a2 = 'too long'
      gosub fail
      return
   end
   if len(convert(marks, '', id)) # len(id) then
      fail.num = 12508 ; fail.a1 = srvname ; fail.a2 = 'holds a mark'
      gosub fail
   end
   return

* ======================================================================
* check.field - the field number in c becomes fld (0 is allowed and answers null)
check.field:
   fld = -1
   if c matches '1N0N' then
      if len(c) <= 6 then fld = c + 0
   end
   if fld < 0 then
      txt = c ; gosub clean.text
      fail.num = 12511 ; fail.a1 = srvname ; fail.a2 = out.txt
      gosub fail
   end
   return

* ======================================================================
* shape.names - srv from a and fname from b, lower case; name.ok and fname.ok say whether each has the shape of a server name / a remote file
* name.  A remote file name is letters, digits and . _ - $ up to MAX.ID.LEN, with the word dict in front for a dictionary.  A slash is not one: no
* pathname can be asked for.
shape.names:
   srv = downcase(trim(a))
   chk.text = srv ; chk.set = lc.set:digits ; gosub check.chars
   name.ok = (len(srv) >= 1 and len(srv) <= 16 and chk.ok)
   if name.ok then name.ok = (index(lc.set, srv[1, 1], 1) # 0)
   fname = downcase(trim(b))
   body = fname
   is.dict = @false
   if fname[1, 5] = 'dict ' then
      is.dict = @true
      body = trim(fname[6, 999])
   end
   chk.text = body ; chk.set = lc.set:digits:'._-$' ; gosub check.chars
   fname.ok = (len(body) >= 1 and len(body) <= MAX.ID.LEN and chk.ok)
   if fname.ok then
      if is.dict then fname = 'dict ' : body
      else fname = body
   end
   return

* ======================================================================
* check.def - host, port, racct and pin (from the definition) have the shape set.server stores.  Sets bad.field to the first that has not.
check.def:
   bad.field = ''
   chk.text = host ; chk.set = lc.set:upcase(lc.set):digits:'.-:_' ; gosub check.chars
   if len(host) < 1 or len(host) > 253 or not(chk.ok) then bad.field = 'host'
   if bad.field = '' then
      if not(port matches '1N0N') or len(port) > 5 then
         bad.field = 'port'
      end else
         if port + 0 < 1 or port + 0 > 65535 then bad.field = 'port'
      end
   end
   if bad.field = '' then
      chk.ok = (len(racct) >= 1 and len(racct) <= 255)
      for k = 1 to len(racct)
         ch = seq(racct[k, 1])
         if ch < 33 or ch > 126 then chk.ok = @false
      next k
      if not(chk.ok) then bad.field = 'account'
   end
   if bad.field = '' then
      chk.text = pin ; chk.set = hex.set ; gosub check.chars
      if len(pin) # 64 or not(chk.ok) then bad.field = 'key'
   end
   return

* ======================================================================
* check.gate - does this session hold the NET gate, or is it the administrator?  Sets gate.ok (and is.admin).  The one place that asks.
check.gate:
   is.admin = kernel(K$ADMINISTRATOR, -1)
   gate.ok = @true
   if not(is.admin) and not(kernel(K$NET, -1)) then gate.ok = @false
   return

* ======================================================================
* find.slot - the slot of the connected server srv, or 0
find.slot:
   slot = 0
   for sx = 1 to NF$MAX.SERVERS
      if nf.s.state(sx) = 1 then
         if nf.s.name(sx) = srv then slot = sx
      end
   next sx
   return

* ======================================================================
* count.up - ns servers connected, nh remote files open (state 1)
count.up:
   ns = 0
   nh = 0
   for sx = 1 to NF$MAX.SERVERS
      if nf.s.state(sx) = 1 then ns += 1
   next sx
   for hy = 1 to NF$MAX.HANDLES
      if nf.h.state(hy) = 1 then nh += 1
   next hy
   return

* ======================================================================
* after.rq - what the request that has just been made did, from the object in obj.  rq.lost: no reply at all (ServerStatus is still the
* mark set before the call).  rq.err: the server answered with an error and obj->error holds its text (cleaned into rq.txt).  rq.st: the
* STATUS() the server sent.
after.rq:
   st = obj->ServerStatus
   rq.lost = (st = NF$NOREPLY)
*  The class reads the status as a SIGNED 32 bit value (oconv 'ILL': -3023 arrives as -3023, measured 18 Oct 26), so a reply CAN be negative and
*  the "lost" test is equality with the mark, not a sign test.  A status above 2147483647 (a class that read it unsigned) is made negative
*  as well.  On a success the status is never read: apisrvr sends whatever the last statement left in STATUS().
   if not(rq.lost) then
      if st > 2147483647 then st -= 4294967296
   end
   rq.st = st
   if rq.lost then rq.st = 0
   txt = obj->error
   rq.err = (txt # '')
   gosub clean.text
   rq.txt = out.txt
   return

* ======================================================================
* request.limits - the waits for a reply, in milliseconds, from the configuration (K8d, 19 Oct 26): req.ms is NETREPLY seconds for each read of a reply (30 by default, 1 to 3600),
* clear.ms the wait for the reply to a CLEARFILE, which is never less than NF$CLEAR.MIN.MS (a big file takes longer than a reply bound) and never less than req.ms.
request.limits:
   req.ms = config('NETREPLY') * 1000
   clear.ms = NF$CLEAR.MIN.MS
   if req.ms > clear.ms then clear.ms = req.ms
   return

* ======================================================================
* on.error.text - the text of an ON ERROR reply (the kind the server's ON ERROR clause gives, and the kind it answers a refusal with), into rq.txt.  The class keeps the
* payload of THIS reply in obj->LastText (message.pair empties it before every request, so it cannot be an earlier reply's), and the server empties its own message at the top
* of every request, so an ON ERROR clause that had nothing to say sends no text and not the previous refusal's.  The text is cleaned here like any text that came from
* another machine.  A reply with no text shows 'status <n>' (rq.st), which is also in rc<6>.  19 Oct 26, K1: this was three places that showed 'status <n>' whatever the
* reply said, so the reason for a refusal (12526 for a files-only login without the write group) never reached the person at the client.
on.error.text:
   txt = obj->LastText
   gosub clean.text
   if out.txt = '' then
      rq.txt = 'status ':rq.st
   end else
      rq.txt = out.txt
   end
   return

* ======================================================================
* lost.fail - the request named in opname got no reply: the connection is dropped, every handle on it is lost, the call answers 12502.  For a
* CHANGING request (write, writeu, delete, deleteu, clear.file: changing is set by act.change) it is not known whether the change was made, so
* the answer is 12514 and the audit line  netfile lost server=<name> op=<operation>.  Nothing is retried, ever.
lost.fail:
   srvname = nf.s.name(slot)
   gosub drop.conn
   if changing then
      void kernel(K$AUDIT, 'netfile lost server=' : srvname : ' op=' : opname)
      fail.num = 12514 ; fail.a1 = srvname ; fail.a2 = opname
   end else
      fail.num = 12502 ; fail.a1 = srvname ; fail.a2 = opname
   end
   gosub fail
   return

* ======================================================================
* rc.locked - rc as NF$RC.LOCKED: another user holds the record; rq.st is the holder's user number (-1 lock table full, -2 deadlock).  Not an
* error: failed is not set.
rc.locked:
   rc = ''
   rc<1> = NF$RC.LOCKED
   rc<6> = rq.st
   return

* ======================================================================
* mode.now - does the definition of the server of handle hx say write NOW?  Sets mode.ok.  A definition that cannot be read, or has gone, or is
* not whole, or says anything but write, is read: fail closed.
mode.now:
   mode.ok = @false
   defs = ''
   err = ''
   call !netdefs('READ', defs, err)
   if err = '' then
      n = dcount(defs, @fm)
      for dx = 1 to n
         if dcount(defs<dx>, @vm) = 8 and defs<dx,1> = nf.h.srv(hx) then
            if defs<dx,6> = 'write' then mode.ok = @true
         end
      next dx
   end
   return

* ======================================================================
* audit.once - the audit line in aud.line, unless the letter aud.k has already been written for handle hx.  The letter is kept in nf.h.aud(hx).
audit.once:
   if index(nf.h.aud(hx), aud.k, 1) = 0 then
      nf.h.aud(hx) = nf.h.aud(hx) : aud.k
      void kernel(K$AUDIT, aud.line)
   end
   return

* ======================================================================
* server.fail - the server answered the request named in opname with an error (12505); rc<6> has the status it sent.
server.fail:
   fail.num = 12505 ; fail.a1 = srvname ; fail.a2 = opname ; fail.a3 = rq.txt
   gosub fail
   rc<6> = rq.st
   return

* ======================================================================
* drop.conn - end the connection in slot: its object is released (its destructor sends a quit and closes the socket), the slot is free, and
* every handle on it becomes lost (state 2).  The object in obj is released too.
drop.conn:
   obj = 0
   if slot >= 1 then
      if slot <= NF$MAX.SERVERS then
         nf.s.obj(slot) = 0
         nf.s.state(slot) = 0
         nf.s.name(slot) = ''
         for hy = 1 to NF$MAX.HANDLES
            if nf.h.state(hy) = 1 then
               if nf.h.slot(hy) = slot then
                  nf.h.state(hy) = 2
                  nf.h.slot(hy) = 0
               end
            end
         next hy
         nf.lost += 1
      end
   end
   return

* ======================================================================
* idle.release - a connection that this very call made, and that has no file, is ended (a login nobody uses is not left on the server).
idle.release:
   if created then
      obj->disconnect
      obj = 0
      nf.s.obj(slot) = 0
      nf.s.state(slot) = 0
      nf.s.name(slot) = ''
   end
   return

* ======================================================================
* free.handle / clear.handle - the entry in hx (free.handle) or hy (clear.handle) back to nothing
free.handle:
   hy = hx
   gosub clear.handle
   return

clear.handle:
   nf.h.state(hy) = 0
   nf.h.slot(hy) = 0
   nf.h.fno(hy) = 0
   nf.h.srv(hy) = ''
   nf.h.file(hy) = ''
   nf.h.acct(hy) = ''
   nf.h.mode(hy) = ''
   nf.h.aud(hy) = ''
   return

* ======================================================================
* fail - rc as an error: fail.num and its arguments.  failed is set so that the chain of checks stops.
fail:
   rc = ''
   rc<1> = NF$RC.ERROR
   rc<2> = fail.num
   rc<3> = fail.a1
   rc<4> = fail.a2
   rc<5> = fail.a3
   failed = @true
   return

* ======================================================================
* rc.else - rc as the ELSE branch, with the remote STATUS() in rc.st
rc.else:
   rc = ''
   rc<1> = NF$RC.ELSE
   rc<6> = rc.st
   return

* ======================================================================
* refuse - an NF$OPEN refused by a local check: rc from fail.num, and the audit line with the reason in refused.why.  Nothing was dialled.
refuse:
   gosub fail
   void kernel(K$AUDIT, 'netfile refused server=' : safe : ' reason=' : refused.why)
   return

* ======================================================================
* check.chars - is every character of chk.text in chk.set?  Sets chk.ok.
check.chars:
   chk.ok = @true
   for k = 1 to len(chk.text)
      if index(chk.set, chk.text[k, 1], 1) = 0 then chk.ok = @false
   next k
   return

* ======================================================================
* clean.text - txt as out.txt: printable ASCII only and at most 100 characters.  Text that came from another machine, or from the caller, is
* shown, never trusted to hold nothing a terminal would act on.
clean.text:
   out.txt = ''
   if len(txt) > 100 then txt = txt[1, 100]
   for k = 1 to len(txt)
      ch = seq(txt[k, 1])
      if ch >= 32 and ch <= 126 then out.txt := txt[k, 1]
   next k
   return

end

